WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.720
It's a new movie in the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;
Universe, so you know it's full of references&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.640
to the comics and movies that have come&nbsp;
before — and maybe the stories to come,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.040
too. These are the Easter eggs you missed in&nbsp;
"Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings."

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.000
Writer/director Destin Daniel Cretton has gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.680
on record saying "Shang-Chi" is&nbsp;
set after the so-called "Blip,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.480
when all of the victims of Thanos' snap&nbsp;
have returned after five long years away.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.960
The timeline is established in the movie by&nbsp;
dialogue and the set dressing; take a close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.840
look at the scene early in "Shang-Chi" when Shaun&nbsp;
visits Katy and her family. As he pulls open the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:39.280
exterior door to her building, you can see a flyer&nbsp;
reading "Post-Blip anxiety? You are not alone."

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.080
This might be the kind of sign that advertised&nbsp;
Steve Rogers' support group that he hosted after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.960
the Snap, as seen in "Avengers: Endgame." Of&nbsp;
course, that scene took place before everyone's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.040
return, and Steve ultimately was giving&nbsp;
advice that he may have ended up regretting:

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.640
"You gotta move on..."

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.120
Yeah, or maybe you have to go back in time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.000
and kick as many asses as it&nbsp;
takes to sort this mess out.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.640
The group advertised in "Shang-Chi" seems to be&nbsp;
not only for those with loved ones who vanished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.040
but also the people who returned after years&nbsp;
away. At the time when "Shang-Chi" is set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.320
anxiety is a common affliction. Now that&nbsp;
androids, aliens, and wizards are commonplace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.080
how can a normal person feel like anything&nbsp;
other than an ant, hoping to not get squashed?

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.120
"An ant-man. That's what they called you."

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.320
In the mid-credits scene, Shang-Chi and Katy have&nbsp;
a conversation with a virtual Captain Marvel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.120
Bruce Banner. Along with Doctor Strange&nbsp;
wingman Wong, the braintrust is extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.080
interested in the Ten Rings, the tale of how&nbsp;
Shang-Chi acquired them, and their true nature.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.640
They debate the Rings' power and origin, with&nbsp;
Carol Danvers calling them unlike any alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.680
tech she's ever seen. Eventually, they seem to&nbsp;
conclude that the Rings are some sort of beacon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.240
sending a message for purposes unknown.&nbsp;
Then Banner signs off by saying:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.120
"Guys, stay safe. Welcome to the circus."

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.000
It's a fun cameo scene that seems to set up&nbsp;
our heroes for future Avengers collaborations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.280
But sharp-eyed viewers might also notice that&nbsp;
Banner's right arm is in a sling. Any guesses why?

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.800
As you recall, in "Avengers: Endgame" it&nbsp;
was determined that only the Hulk was strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.760
enough to wear the Stark Gauntlet and snap&nbsp;
everyone back into existence. But in doing so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:21.120
he was exposing himself to massive gamma&nbsp;
radiation, and sure enough, he fried his arm.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.080
In a 2019 interview with ComicBook.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.480
"Endgame" director Joe Russo said the&nbsp;
damage is permanent. According to him,

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.280
"He's lost an arm ... that's not coming back.&nbsp;
He's damaged himself. It's permanent damage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.200
the same way there was permanent&nbsp;
damage with Thanos. It's irreversible."

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.280
So, will Bruce Banner spend the rest of&nbsp;
his MCU games with his arm in a sling?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.680
In a world where Thor can pop in a new eye and&nbsp;
Rhodey can become paralyzed from a fractured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.760
spinal column but walk again with help from Stark&nbsp;
bionic supports, no injury seems truly permanent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.280
We'll see how long Ruffalo has to&nbsp;
keep wearing the sling, but in this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.040
his first Hulk scene since "Endgame,"&nbsp;
it's clear that he's still in a bad way.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.840
Sharp-eyed fans were delighted when, earlier&nbsp;
this summer, they seemed to uncover a quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.560
shot of Abomination in the "Shang-Chi" trailer.&nbsp;
Sure enough, when our newly-revealed hero makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.800
his way back to China in an attempt to locate&nbsp;
his sister Xialing, he looks for her at The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.440
Golden Daggers Club and discovers that not only&nbsp;
does she run the place, but currently appearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.360
on the main stage is a smackdown between&nbsp;
the "Incredible Hulk" villain and Wong.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.120
For starters, there are several things&nbsp;
worth discussing about Emil Blonsky's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.720
monstrous appearance. First, he seems&nbsp;
to be evolving, or, at the very least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.520
the CGI is getting better — after all, it&nbsp;
has been 13 years since we last saw the guy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.440
He now has ear fins that look fully&nbsp;
engaged, and he boasts more prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.560
dorsal spine needles — both signatures that make&nbsp;
Abomination stand out on the comic book page.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.000
The second interesting thing worth noting&nbsp;
is that the character looks even less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.040
like Tim Roth than he did before&nbsp;
— which is saying something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.680
because he has never really looked&nbsp;
much like him to begin with.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.840
Over the last decade, Marvel fans have watched&nbsp;
as Hulk has increasingly resembled the actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.320
who played him, with storylines that sought&nbsp;
to humanize the monster within Bruce Banner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.680
But while "The Incredible Hulk" gave&nbsp;
us an Abomination whose face at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.840
somewhat resembled a human's reactions,&nbsp;
the Abomination character design now seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.080
to be moving in the opposite direction&nbsp;
of Hulk, leaving all humanity behind.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.200
"You have to explain that statement, sir."

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.320
"No, I don't."

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.480
Perhaps the most notable thing about this&nbsp;
Abomination, however, might be his shift in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.760
tone. As you may recall, after Abomination nearly&nbsp;
defeated Hulk in Harlem, General Thunderbolt Ross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.200
had the creature locked up in a high-tech cell&nbsp;
in Alaska; Phil Coulson said in a 2014 episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.000
of "Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D." that he was still in&nbsp;
that cryo-cell. But in the 2011 "One-Shot" short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.360
film "The Consultant," an angry Coulson says the&nbsp;
World Security Council wants Abomination released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.240
and assigned to ... the Avengers initiative?&nbsp;
Coulson says that the Council considers Blonsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.440
to be a war hero — they want him exonerated,&nbsp;
released, and added to the Avengers roster.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.600
In a conclusion played for laughs, S.H.I.E.L.D.&nbsp;
reveals in the short film that they purposefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.000
sent Tony Stark to screw up the negotiations —&nbsp;
leading to the "Incredible Hulk" post-credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.360
cameo where he clashed with a hard-drinking Ross&nbsp;
— and as a result, Blonsky went back into custody.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.160
"Mission accomplished. The Abomination&nbsp;
will remain in his cage for now."

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.120
But clearly, Blonsky is not only on the loose&nbsp;
post-Blip, but he seems to be chummy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.680
Wong — who "Shang-Chi" reveals to be a major&nbsp;
player in the recruitment of new Avengers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.240
Could it be that (whatever remnants remain of)&nbsp;
the World Security Council finally got their way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.720
and Abomination will soon be fighting on the&nbsp;
same side as Hulk? Abomination is gearing up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:32.080
to appear more frequently in the Marvel sandbox,&nbsp;
including the upcoming "She-Hulk" Disney+ series.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:33.680
"How do you feel?"

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:37.840
"Pissed off and ready for round three."

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.880
The first real indication to Shaun&nbsp;
that his reality is about to get very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:46.960
very expanded is when he and Katy&nbsp;
are attacked on a San Francisco bus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:52.160
by several highly-trained men — one of them&nbsp;
wielding a machete in place of his right forearm.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.880
As well-read comic fans will be eager to point&nbsp;
out, his name is Razor Fist, and you wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:02.640
want to try and shake his hand. In the comics,&nbsp;
Razor Fist was introduced in 1975 in "Master of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:08.720
Kung Fu" #29 as a henchman of the evil Carlton&nbsp;
Velcro. This villain had a razor-sharp blade on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:13.920
each arm — not that it helped him much, as he died&nbsp;
three issues later, shot down by Velcro's guards.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:18.960
Of course, no comics mantle, however obscure,&nbsp;
is ever laid down for good. Velcro attempted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.480
create more Razor Fists in 1981 — when brothers&nbsp;
William and Douglas Scott were in a car accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:29.040
he gave them sword arms, too; Douglas died&nbsp;
and William became the new Razor Fist.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.200
In more recent years, that Razor Fist has&nbsp;
appeared in various comics, usually seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.800
as supporting muscle for supervillains&nbsp;
— much like we find him in "Shang-Chi."

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:40.400
In the background of almost&nbsp;
every scene containing Wenwu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.720
you may have noticed a silent man dressed&nbsp;
in black, wearing white make-up punctuated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.360
by streaks of red and black. That guy's&nbsp;
name is Death Dealer, and for good reason.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.280
In the comics, the character's real name&nbsp;
is Li Ching-Lin, and like other characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.160
in Shang-Chi's orbit, he has a history with&nbsp;
MI-6. The baddie was actually a double agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.440
working on the sly for Fu Manchu&nbsp;
when he and Shang-Chi had an epic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.280
battle that nearly killed him — and he&nbsp;
then took on the Death Dealer persona.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.440
In "Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.520
this Death Dealer has clearly been with&nbsp;
Wenwu for multiple decades — and seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.280
have largely led the training that taught&nbsp;
Shaun every possible way to kill a man.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.200
The mysterious nature of the live-action&nbsp;
character, coupled with the lack of dialogue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.720
has given rise to some interesting&nbsp;
theories about Death Dealer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.400
whose story will certainly be&nbsp;
explored in future movies in the MCU.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.360
Okay, technically this Easter egg isn't in&nbsp;
"Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:34.880
but you'll likely glimpse it in one of&nbsp;
the trailers that plays before the film.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.600
In the multiverse-expanding&nbsp;
first trailer for "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.680
No Way Home," Dr. Stephen Strange&nbsp;
makes the really, really bad decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.000
to cast a spell that will make the entire&nbsp;
world forget that Peter Parker is Spider-Man.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.160
Under normal circumstances, an MCU&nbsp;
fan would say: "No way. Wong would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:53.920
never let such an irresponsible thing&nbsp;
happen. He's the voice of reason."

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:58.720
Great point. But when the cat's away, the mice&nbsp;
will play. So, while we see Wong in the trailer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:03.680
instructing Strange not to mess with the spell,&nbsp;
we also see him in a winter parka, holding two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:08.800
suitcases and traveling somewhere that looks like&nbsp;
it has old, regal structures adorned by columns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:14.160
and domes. Which is when Strange casts his spell&nbsp;
on Peter's behalf, and all hell breaks loose.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.320
Those who've seen "Shang-Chi" know that&nbsp;
Wong pops up multiple times in China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:23.360
as well as San Francisco. So, could either of&nbsp;
those be where he's teleporting to in the trailer?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:27.760
Do the events of that scene run concurrent&nbsp;
to "Shang-Chi"? We'll find out soon enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:31.120
once Spidey's latest adventure&nbsp;
slings itself into theaters.

